<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brandenburg an der Havel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brenna, Brennabor</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> is a city on the Havel in the German state of Brandenburg, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. It began as Brenna, the chief stronghold of the Slavic Hevelli, where Otto I founded a bishopric in 948. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a> secured it in 1157, and it gave its name to the March of Brandenburg and later to the modern state. Its cathedral island preserves medieval buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brandenburg-an-der-havel.jpg" alt="Brandenburg an der Havel" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Queryzo; derivative work by Regi51 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Johannis_in_Brandenburg_an_der_Havel_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> an der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> lies on the banks of the River Havel, which is navigable here as a European Waterway. The name combines two Slavic words, braniti, to protect or defend, and bor, forest or wood. The town gave its name to the medieval Bishopric of Brandenburg, the Margraviate of Brandenburg and the present German state, and it was the original nucleus of the realms of Brandenburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. It was capital of the Margraviate until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> replaced it in 1417.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Brenna was a Slavic fort, conquered in 929 after the Battle of Lenzen by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler of Saxony</a>, and first mentioned in documents then as Brennaburg. At the Magdeburg Assembly of Princes in 948 the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and Havelburg were established under the control of Otto I. A major Slavic uprising in 983 returned the area east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> to Slavic chiefs of the Hevelli until the middle of the 12th century. The last of them, Pribislav, died in 1150.</p><p>His widow Petrissa enabled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert I of Brandenburg</a> to take over Brenna. Jaksa of Kopanica drove him out, and he returned in 1157 after a long siege, by agreement and with a joint German and Slav garrison, and styled himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>. By 1160 nobles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> and peasants from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> were settling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spree">Spree</a> basin. The foundation stone of a cathedral was laid on the cathedral island in 1165, and it was consecrated five years later. In 1314–1315 the Old and New Towns joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. Plundering and destruction in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> (1618–1648) led the court to leave. The two towns merged in 1715.</p><p>In the late 19th century the town became an industrial centre, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, toys and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a> under brands such as Brennabor. In 1933–1934 one of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-concentration-camps">Nazi concentration camps</a> stood here. The Brandenburg-Görden Prison later became a euthanasia centre where the Nazis murdered people with psychiatric disorders, including children, in Action T4, and it was one of the first places where they experimented with killing by gas. Heavy bombing, partly because of the Arado aircraft factory, destroyed 70 percent of the city by 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Dominsel, the cathedral island, is its historic heart, with the Cathedral of St Peter and Paul, begun in the Romanesque style in 1165 and completed as a Gothic cathedral in the 14th century; the Wagner organ of 1725 is one of the best-known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> organs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Other sights are the brick Gothic Katharinenkirche of 1401, the Old Town Hall with its statue of the knight Roland of 1474, and parts of the medieval wall with four watchtowers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brandenburg an der Havel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Lothair III gave the Northern March to Albert the Bear of the House of Ascania; Albert had an understanding with Pribislav-Henry, the Christian prince of the Hevelli, who made him his heir, and on Pribislav’s death in 1150 he took over the fortress of <b>Brandenburg an der Havel</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Otto I founded the bishopric of Brandenburg in 948 at the captured Hevelli stronghold of <b>Brenna</b>, as part of his eastern mission, placing it under Magdeburg in 968.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-brandenburg">Prince-Bishopric of Brandenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>Their chief stronghold was <b>Brenna</b>, the later Brandenburg an der Havel.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-the-hevelli">Principality of the Hevelli</a></li></ul></section>
